WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.200
Welcome back to Building
Ticket-to-Machine.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.312
This is Chapter 5: the re-read
rule, the kit's Build 2.

3
00:00:06.312 --> 00:00:08.524
A success response is not closure.

4
00:00:08.524 --> 00:00:12.750
Neither is a ticket status, and neither
is a comparison against nothing.

5
00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:17.575
A finding closes only when a re-read of
the target shows the intended state.

6
00:00:17.575 --> 00:00:22.612
A ServiceNow business rule enforces that,
and a Python client does the re-reading.

7
00:00:22.612 --> 00:00:27.081
This chapter adds an offline mode so you
can watch every decision on a laptop,

8
00:00:27.081 --> 00:00:29.700
and a guard against closing on nothing.

9
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:34.962
Why does this rule exist?

10
00:00:34.962 --> 00:00:38.787
Because there are several ways to close
a finding without proving anything.

11
00:00:38.787 --> 00:00:41.085
The first is closure by ticket status:

12
00:00:41.085 --> 00:00:45.309
SailPoint's Service Desk integration
treats the mapped ServiceNow

13
00:00:45.309 --> 00:00:49.163
status as completion, and a person
can simply mark the work done.

14
00:00:49.163 --> 00:00:51.487
That's the example guide's first gap.

15
00:00:51.487 --> 00:00:56.714
The others are subtler: comparing against
an intended state that names nothing,

16
00:00:56.714 --> 00:01:00.357
which always matches; trusting
a read older than the fix;

17
00:01:00.357 --> 00:01:02.812
and treating a missing read as a match.

18
00:01:02.812 --> 00:01:05.140
This chapter closes all four.

19
00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:10.965
Here's the rule inside ServiceNow.

20
00:01:10.965 --> 00:01:14.040
It runs before any update
that changes the state.

21
00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:18.886
If someone sets a closed state, the record
goes to awaiting verification instead,

22
00:01:18.886 --> 00:01:21.706
unless that same update
carries a new evidence

23
00:01:21.706 --> 00:01:24.165
hash and comes from the verifier account.

24
00:01:24.165 --> 00:01:28.803
Evidence alone isn't enough, and
the verifier alone isn't enough.

25
00:01:28.803 --> 00:01:33.455
There's one human path: a named exception
approver can cancel with a close note,

26
00:01:33.455 --> 00:01:36.799
which is the Federal
Modernization Workbook's Book 13's

27
00:01:36.799 --> 00:01:39.852
declared-exception lane,
and it stays on the record.

28
00:01:39.852 --> 00:01:44.280
The rule also governs tickets the
Service Desk integration opened.

29
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.455
The other half is the re-read client.

30
00:01:50.455 --> 00:01:55.463
It lists waiting findings, re-reads each
subject according to its system, an

31
00:01:55.463 --> 00:01:57.886
account, an identity or an Entra user,

32
00:01:57.886 --> 00:02:00.955
and compares the read
with the intended state.

33
00:02:00.955 --> 00:02:05.254
A match closes the record with
the evidence: the query, the time,

34
00:02:05.254 --> 00:02:10.818
a hash of the response, the intended state
and the checker, attached as JSON too.

35
00:02:10.818 --> 00:02:12.292
A difference reopens it.

36
00:02:12.292 --> 00:02:15.804
Because SailPoint's copy
reflects the last aggregation,

37
00:02:15.804 --> 00:02:19.167
a read older than the wait is
pending for a grace period.

38
00:02:19.167 --> 00:02:22.170
And nothing is written
without the apply flag.

39
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:27.158
Two things were missing.

40
00:02:27.158 --> 00:02:28.571
The first was this guard.

41
00:02:28.571 --> 00:02:32.261
The client already skipped a record
with an empty intended state,

42
00:02:32.261 --> 00:02:36.704
but not one whose intended state was a
non-empty object naming nothing, which is

43
00:02:36.704 --> 00:02:41.283
exactly what ServiceNow stores for a native
change the workflow didn't describe.

44
00:02:41.283 --> 00:02:44.288
Comparing a read against
that finds no difference,

45
00:02:44.288 --> 00:02:48.295
so the client would have closed the
record, with evidence, on nothing.

46
00:02:48.295 --> 00:02:51.381
Now Chapter 1's check runs
before anything is read,

47
00:02:51.381 --> 00:02:55.430
and such a record is skipped
with the reason, never verified.

48
00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:02.992
The second thing missing was a way to
watch the decisions without a tenant.

49
00:03:02.992 --> 00:03:07.762
The offline mode takes saved waiting
records in the Table API's shape,

50
00:03:07.762 --> 00:03:10.942
saved reads keyed by
subject, and a fixed clock.

51
00:03:10.942 --> 00:03:15.367
The same function makes the same
decisions; only the reader is swapped.

52
00:03:15.367 --> 00:03:18.475
It's a dry run only: the
apply flag is refused,

53
00:03:18.475 --> 00:03:24.155
and a subject with no saved read is an
error, because a missing read isn't a match.

54
00:03:24.155 --> 00:03:27.548
Six fictional waiting
records cover every outcome,

55
00:03:27.548 --> 00:03:32.330
from a clean verify to the empty
intended state the guard exists for.

56
00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.092
The tests.

57
00:03:37.092 --> 00:03:42.029
Six new offline tests cover the six
outcomes, the grace period expiring,

58
00:03:42.029 --> 00:03:46.631
the guard never calling the reader,
a missing saved read as an error,

59
00:03:46.631 --> 00:03:49.392
a null read as not
found, and the refusals.

60
00:03:49.392 --> 00:03:52.755
The kit's original nine
re-read tests still pass.

61
00:03:52.755 --> 00:03:56.223
And the business rule's six
Node tests pin its behavior:

62
00:03:56.223 --> 00:04:01.233
a person's close is redirected, only the
verifier with fresh evidence may close,

63
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:06.012
either one alone isn't enough, the
exception approver may cancel with a note,

64
00:04:06.012 --> 00:04:10.310
and on incidents only the
integration's tickets are governed.

65
00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:18.035
The facts each re-read depends
on, read on November first.

66
00:04:18.035 --> 00:04:20.797
The Table API lists and patches records,

67
00:04:20.797 --> 00:04:26.322
and the Attachment API uploads the evidence
file with a mandatory content type.

68
00:04:26.322 --> 00:04:29.767
An account is re-read through
SailPoint's Accounts API,

69
00:04:29.767 --> 00:04:34.667
an identity through Search on the
identities index, and an Entra user through

70
00:04:34.667 --> 00:04:39.260
Graph's get-a-user call, which returns
not found when the user doesn't exist.

71
00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:42.500
And SailPoint's own best-practice
page confirms the gap:

72
00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:47.500
the mapped ServiceNow status decides
when it treats provisioning as complete.

73
00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.450
Three points are open.

74
00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:55.351
The biggest: which user
ServiceNow records for a

75
00:04:55.351 --> 00:04:58.325
call made with an OAuth
client-credentials token.

76
00:04:58.325 --> 00:05:02.762
The rule compares exactly that with its
verifier property, so if they differ

77
00:05:02.762 --> 00:05:06.987
it refuses every close, and people
start looking for a way around the rule.

78
00:05:06.987 --> 00:05:09.099
Lab 2 checks it before go-live.

79
00:05:09.099 --> 00:05:13.414
Also open: how long a SailPoint read
lags the source, which depends on the

80
00:05:13.414 --> 00:05:16.046
aggregation schedule, and how to recognize

81
00:05:16.046 --> 00:05:19.200
a ticket the Service
Desk integration opened.

82
00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:26.275
For the I S S O, this is
the closure evidence.

83
00:05:26.275 --> 00:05:31.171
A finding closed only after a re-read
showed the intended state, with the query,

84
00:05:31.171 --> 00:05:35.149
the time, a hash of the response
and the checker, which is Book 13's

85
00:05:35.149 --> 00:05:39.662
fourth clause and supports change
control and continuous monitoring.

86
00:05:39.662 --> 00:05:44.625
No person can close a finding by setting
its state, which is separation of duties.

87
00:05:44.625 --> 00:05:47.125
A read that predates the
change doesn't count.

88
00:05:47.125 --> 00:05:50.822
And an empty intended state
never closes anything:

89
00:05:50.822 --> 00:05:53.920
a verified change, not an asserted one.

90
00:05:57.670 --> 00:05:58.708
Who owns this?

91
00:05:58.708 --> 00:06:01.419
Developers who own the client or the rule;

92
00:06:01.419 --> 00:06:05.726
ServiceNow administrators who
configure the rule's properties,

93
00:06:05.726 --> 00:06:10.512
the waiting state's value, the verifier's
user name, and the exception role;

94
00:06:10.512 --> 00:06:13.782
and the I S S O, for whom
this is the closure evidence.

95
00:06:13.782 --> 00:06:15.345
The identities are simple.

96
00:06:15.345 --> 00:06:20.157
The verifier can close a finding only
in the same update as fresh evidence.

97
00:06:20.157 --> 00:06:23.046
A named exception approver
can cancel with a note.

98
00:06:23.046 --> 00:06:28.380
Anyone else who sets a closed state just
moves the record to awaiting verification.

99
00:06:32.130 --> 00:06:32.942
Lab 1.

100
00:06:32.942 --> 00:06:34.543
Fifteen tests pass.

101
00:06:34.543 --> 00:06:38.567
Then re-read the six waiting
findings at noon on November first.

102
00:06:38.567 --> 00:06:40.705
Three verify and would close.

103
00:06:40.705 --> 00:06:44.712
One mismatches: a terminated user
is still enabled in Entra ID,

104
00:06:44.712 --> 00:06:47.305
so it would reopen with that difference.

105
00:06:47.305 --> 00:06:51.705
One is pending: the SailPoint account
was last modified before the finding

106
00:06:51.705 --> 00:06:56.317
started waiting, so the read predates the
change and the client leaves it alone.

107
00:06:56.317 --> 00:07:00.380
And one is skipped, because its
intended state names nothing.

108
00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:03.000
That's the case the guard exists for.

109
00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:08.938
Now run the same records a day later.

110
00:07:08.938 --> 00:07:12.249
The pending one has passed its
twenty-four-hour grace period.

111
00:07:12.249 --> 00:07:14.295
The account still isn't disabled,

112
00:07:14.295 --> 00:07:17.325
so it's now a mismatch, and
the record would reopen.

113
00:07:17.325 --> 00:07:20.675
A stale read can't hold
a finding open forever.

114
00:07:20.675 --> 00:07:24.387
The results file keeps each
outcome with its evidence hash.

115
00:07:24.387 --> 00:07:27.538
Finally, run the business
rule's six Node tests.

116
00:07:27.538 --> 00:07:30.321
They pass: a person's close is redirected,

117
00:07:30.321 --> 00:07:34.310
and only the verifier with
fresh evidence can close.

118
00:07:38.060 --> 00:07:40.246
Lab 2 is on your developer instance,

119
00:07:40.246 --> 00:07:43.185
never production, and the
authors haven't run it.

120
00:07:43.185 --> 00:07:45.610
Configure the rule and its properties.

121
00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:50.960
Close a test finding as an ordinary user
and watch it land in awaiting verification.

122
00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:54.386
Then the important step: as
the verifier's OAuth client,

123
00:07:54.386 --> 00:07:58.497
make one harmless update and see
which user ServiceNow recorded.

124
00:07:58.497 --> 00:08:02.961
If it isn't the verifier property,
the rule will refuse every close.

125
00:08:02.961 --> 00:08:06.075
Then give a test finding
a checkable intended

126
00:08:06.075 --> 00:08:09.272
state and run the client,
dry first, then for real.

127
00:08:09.272 --> 00:08:10.640
Back it all out.

128
00:08:14.390 --> 00:08:17.727
The dangers line up with the
ways to close without proof.

129
00:08:17.727 --> 00:08:21.053
Closure by ticket status
is redirected by the rule.

130
00:08:21.053 --> 00:08:24.528
An intended state that names
nothing is skipped by the guard.

131
00:08:24.528 --> 00:08:28.916
A stale read is pending inside the
grace period and a mismatch after it.

132
00:08:28.916 --> 00:08:31.710
A missing read is an error, never a match;

133
00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:35.290
the live reader raises on
anything but a not-found.

134
00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:40.303
A misconfigured verifier would refuse
every close, so check it in Lab 2.

135
00:08:40.303 --> 00:08:44.120
And evidence edited after closure
no longer proves anything,

136
00:08:44.120 --> 00:08:47.090
which is why Chapter
7 recomputes the hash.

137
00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:52.040
Done looks like this.

138
00:08:52.040 --> 00:08:54.368
The rule is active on the finding table,

139
00:08:54.368 --> 00:08:59.315
and on incidents and request items if the
Service Desk integration opens tickets.

140
00:08:59.315 --> 00:09:04.015
The verifier property matches the
user ServiceNow actually records.

141
00:09:04.015 --> 00:09:07.803
The grace period is at least the
longest aggregation interval.

142
00:09:07.803 --> 00:09:11.862
A dry run over this week's waiting
findings skips nothing for an

143
00:09:11.862 --> 00:09:15.765
empty intended state, or each
such record has gone to a person.

144
00:09:15.765 --> 00:09:18.903
Closed records carry their
evidence and attachment.

145
00:09:18.903 --> 00:09:20.316
And the tests pass.

146
00:09:20.316 --> 00:09:23.853
Next, Chapter 6: drift reconciliation.

147
00:09:23.853 --> 00:09:25.010
Thanks for watching.
